# The RFC 4475 corpus is bit-exact test data. SIP is a CRLF protocol and several cases turn
# on exact octets — a trailing space on a request line, an escaped NUL, a bare CR. Git's
# end-of-line normalization would silently rewrite them and every parser test would then be
# asserting against the wrong bytes. Never normalize these.
crates/sipx-testkit/corpus/rfc4475/*.dat -text

# Same reasoning for any future protocol fixture.
*.dat -text
*.sip -text

# Reproducible interoperability captures are sealed byte-for-byte in their run manifest. Peer
# diagnostics can carry meaningful trailing spaces, so preserve those bytes and do not report them
# as source whitespace errors.
docs/comparison/interop/**/runs/**/*.log -text whitespace=-blank-at-eol
